---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import NewsCards from "../components/NewsCards.astro";
import GlossaryTerm from "../components/GlossaryTerm.astro";
import UpcomingSessions from "../components/UpcomingSessions.astro";
import heroIndex from "../assets/images/hero-index.avif";
import heroWorkshops from "../assets/images/hero-workshops.avif";
import headshotBen from "../assets/images/headshot-ben-4.avif";
import heroFundamentals from "../assets/images/hero-fundamentals.avif";
import heroHowModelsUnderstand from "../assets/images/hero-how-models-understand.avif";
import heroLlmVsAi from "../assets/images/hero-llm-vs-ai.avif";
import heroIntro from "../assets/images/hero-intro.avif";
import heroContact from "../assets/images/hero-contact.avif";
---

<BaseLayout title="LLMs Unplugged">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-content">
      <h1 class="hero-title">
        <span class="hero-name">LLMs Unplugged</span>
        <span class="hero-text"
          >Understand AI by building it from scratch, with pen, paper, and dice</span
        >
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-tagline">
        Hands-on teaching resources that demonstrate how large language models
        (LLMs) work&mdash;no computers or coding required.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a href="/lessons/" class="hero-button primary">Get started</a>
        <a href="/about" class="hero-button secondary">Learn more</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-image">
      <Image
        src={heroIndex}
        alt="Workshop participants building language models with pen and paper"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <UpcomingSessions />

  {
    /* a div, not <main>: the page already renders inside the theme's <main id="main"> */
  }
  <div class="home-content">
    <p class="intro-text">
      <em>LLMs Unplugged</em> is a set of hands-on teaching resources that demonstrate
      how large language models work&mdash;no computers or coding required. You count
      word patterns in a text, record them as tally marks on grid paper or physical
      piles of paper cutouts, then roll dice to generate new text from those patterns.
      After building a <GlossaryTerm id="language-model"
        >language model</GlossaryTerm
      > by hand, something clicks: you see that tools like <GlossaryTerm
        id="chatgpt">Claude and ChatGPT</GlossaryTerm
      > work using the same basic principles, just at a vastly larger scale.
    </p>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroWorkshops}
          alt="Diverse group of learners from students to professionals"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Who is this for?</h2>
        <p>
          Although these activities are called "lessons" grouped into "topics",
          they're not just for school classrooms. These activities are suitable
          for audiences from primary school age through to adults&mdash;we've
          delivered them to hundreds of participants including school students,
          undergraduate students, and senior executives from both industry and
          government. Zero coding background required, and no maths beyond basic
          counting and percentages.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even if you already know a bit (or a lot!) about how LLMs work, you'll
          have fun making one and sharing it with others&mdash;and the hands-on
          process leaves you with a more useful mental model than any high-level
          explainer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What's new in LLMs Unplugged?</h2>
      <NewsCards limit={2} />
      <p><a href="/news/">See all news &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image src={headshotBen} alt="Ben Swift" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Who made this?</h2>
        <p>
          <em>LLMs Unplugged</em> is a <a
            href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au/cybernetic-studio/"
            >Cybernetic Studio</a
          > project created by <a href="https://benswift.me">Dr. Ben Swift</a> (PhD
          Computer Science, 2012). But if you're worried that <em>you'll</em> need
          a PhD in Computer Science to understand it then rest assured&mdash;the whole
          point of these resources is to demonstrate that you don't. Which leads to
          the question&hellip;
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroFundamentals}
          alt="Simple counting and dice activities"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>What prior knowledge do I need?</h2>
        <p>
          For <strong>instructors</strong>, the reading and numeracy skills
          required to teach this stuff are really only primary-school level.
          This isn't just for specialist maths teachers or tertiary educators,
          although it works great in those contexts too. For <strong
            >participants</strong
          >, really all you need is a curiosity about how AI systems actually
          work and a willingness to engage with hands-on activities.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each <a href="/lessons/">lesson</a> includes instructor notes and discussion
          questions, plus interactive widgets (which you can use to get your head
          around the concepts) and&mdash;coming soon&mdash;explainer videos you can
          use in class. We also run teacher training sessions and offer professional
          delivery services if you'd like support bringing these activities to your
          organisation or classroom.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroHowModelsUnderstand}
          alt="Understanding AI through hands-on experience"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Why does this matter?</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://chatgpt.com/">ChatGPT</a> arrived in November 2022 and
          suddenly (most) everyone's using <GlossaryTerm id="language-model"
            >LLMs</GlossaryTerm
          >. Yet most people have no real mental model of what's actually
          happening under the hood. They've heard the hand-wave-y and
          mystical-sounding explanations, maybe picked up some vague notions
          about "neural networks" and "training data", but the core mechanism
          remains opaque.
        </p>
        <p>
          Understanding how these systems actually work&mdash;not through
          metaphor or handwaving but through direct experience&mdash;is what
          we're all about. In a world where everyone uses LLMs, this kind of
          understanding isn't optional anymore.
        </p>
        <p>
          These resources don't tell you or your students what to think about
          the role of AI (good/mid/cooked) in society. They provide a grounding
          for
          <em>you</em> to have that discussion; one that's informed not by folktales
          about what might be happening under the hood but by a lived experience of
          doing it for yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroLlmVsAi}
          alt="Understanding the relationship between LLMs and AI"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Why do you keep saying LLMs&mdash;this is AI, right?</h2>
        <p>
          Honestly, the short answer (these days) is yes. The long answer is
          more complicated&mdash;AI is a term that has been applied to many
          different algorithmic techniques and systems over the years. But today
          (in the mid-2020s) Large Language Models (LLMs) have sucked all the
          air out of the room when it comes to AI, so that's mostly what we're
          talking about here. We do like to use the term LLM rather than AI (or
          even "GenAI") to describe these systems because it's a bit clearer
          what we're talking about.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroIntro}
          alt="Getting started with language model activities"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Where should I start?</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the <a href="/lessons/#fundamentals"
            >"Fundamentals" lessons</a
          >. These cover the essential concepts for building and using language
          models: training a bigram model and generating text. After completing
          these lessons, you'll understand the fundamental mechanism behind
          LLM-based AI systems.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-with-image">
      <div class="section-image">
        <Image
          src={heroContact}
          alt="Connecting and sharing ideas"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          If you have questions, success stories about using these materials, or
          would like to discuss adaptations or improvements you've made, get in
          touch at <a href="mailto:ben.swift@anu.edu.au">ben.swift@anu.edu.au</a
          >. If you'd like to hear about what cool stuff is going on at the
          School of Cybernetics, then sign up for the <a
            href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au/#subscribe-to-our-mailing-list-1"
            >mailing list</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    /* Reduced bottom padding sets the gap above the upcoming-sessions box;
       .home-content's top padding mirrors it below for even spacing. */
    padding: 4rem 1.5rem 3rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (min-width: 960px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      padding: 6rem 1.5rem 3rem;
    }
  }

  .hero-name {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #fff;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .hero-title {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .hero-text {
    display: block;
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 8vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .hero-tagline {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .hero-image img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .hero-button {
    padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      color 0.15s,
      background-color 0.15s,
      border-color 0.15s;
  }

  .hero-button.primary {
    background: var(--at-accent);
    color: #fff;
  }

  .hero-button.primary:hover {
    background: var(--at-accent-hover);
  }

  .hero-button.secondary {
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    color: var(--at-text);
  }

  .hero-button.secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--at-accent);
  }

  .home-content {
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.5rem 4rem;
  }

  .intro-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
</style>
